ns like image pull errors, startup failures, or other issues. \n\nBut the user has already used get pods, and the next logical step is to check the logs. So, the tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the logs command for one of the failing pods. For example, checking the frontend pod\'s logs. \n\nWait, the user might need to check multiple pods, but since each tool call is a single command, they might need to do this iteratively. However, the user can only make one tool call per step. So, the first step would be to check the logs of one of the failing pods to get more information. \n\nTherefore, the correct tool choice is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl logs <pod-name> -n hotel-reservation" for one of the non-ready pods, like frontend-78986558db-2txmx. This will retrieve the logs and help identify the root cause.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The pods for frontend, geo, profile, rate, recommendation, reservation, search, and user are in a RunContainerError state. To identify the specific error causing the containers to fail, I need to inspect their logs. The command "kubectl logs <pod-name> -n hotel-reservation" will retrieve the container logs for a specific pod, which can reveal errors like image pull failures, configuration issues, or runtime exceptions. Starting with the frontend pod, which is critical for user interaction, will help pinpoint the root cause.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl logs frontend-78986558db-2txmx -n hotel-reservation"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=840, prompt_tokens=4140, total_tokens=4980, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfac0-e9ed-7f31-862d-795587b97622-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_call