e op=Exists for 300s\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\nEvents:\n  Type     Reason     Age                From               Message\n  ----     ------     ----               ----               -------\n  Normal   Scheduled  68s                default-scheduler  Successfully assigned hotel-reservation/frontend-78986558db-2txmx to node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\n  Normal   Pulled     12s (x3 over 54s)  kubelet            spec.containers{hotel-reserv-frontend}: Container image "jackcuii/hotel-reservation:latest" already present on machine\n  Normal   Created    8s (x3 over 41s)   kubelet            spec.containers{hotel-reserv-frontend}: Created container: hotel-reserv-frontend\n  Warning  Failed     5s (x3 over 35s)   kubelet            spec.containers{hotel-reserv-frontend}: Error: failed to start container "hotel-reserv-frontend": Error response from daemon: failed to create task for container: failed to create shim task: OCI runtime create failed: runc create failed: unable to start container process: error during container init: exec: "frontend": executable file not found in $PATH\n  Warning  BackOff    3s                 kubelet            spec.containers{hotel-reserv-frontend}: Back-off restarting failed container hotel-reserv-frontend in pod frontend-78986558db-2txmx_hotel-reservation(61e86933-057b-46f3-8232-bd9f36e3b74c)\n', id='b77e271a-3421-4491-a2cf-2aa1a9c08a44', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-80d5e809a5ad9f32'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b30095df-538b-4920-90b7-47b346688daf'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the frontend pod is in a RunContainerError state. From the previous steps, we know that the container failed because the executable "frontend" wasn\'t found. The error message says "exec: "frontend": executable file not found in $PATH". \n\nHmm, so the container is trying to run a command called "frontend", but it\'s not present. The image used is "jackcuii/hotel-reservation:latest". Maybe the image doesn\'t have the frontend binary installed, or the entry point is misconfigured. \n\nWait, the user mentioned the application is built with Go and gRPC. Maybe the frontend service is a Go application, but the container image might not have the correct entry point set. Or perhaps the image is built incorrectly, missing the executable. \n\nI should check the Dockerfile of the frontend image to see how the executable is built. But since I can\'t directly access the Dockerfile, maybe I can use the kubectl command to describe the pod and check the container\'s command and args. Wait, the user already did a describe pod, and the command is listed as "frontend". \n\nAlternatively, maybe the image is not properly built. The image name is "jackcuii/hotel-reservation:latest". Maybe the image doesn\'t include the frontend binary. Or perhaps the entry point in the Dockerfile is set to a different name, like "./frontend" or something else. \n\nAnother possibility is that the container\'s PATH environment variable doesn\'t include the directory where the executable is located. But the error specifically says the executable isn\'t found in $PATH, so maybe the binary is not in the PATH or the image is missing it. \n\nSince the user can\'t directly inspect the image, maybe they need to check the image\'s contents. But with the available tools, they can\'t do that. Wait, the user has the "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd" tool. Maybe they can exec into the container to check, but the pod is in a failed state. Wait, the pod is in Running state but the container is in an error. \n\nWait, the pod status is Running, but the container is in Waiting state with the error. So maybe the container can\'t start because the entry point is wrong. The command specified is "frontend", but the image might not have that executable. \n\nSo the root cause is likely that the container image doesn\'t contain the "frontend" executable, or the entry point is misconfigured. The user needs to check the Dockerfile or the build process of the frontend image. But since the tools available don\'t include checking the image\'s contents, maybe the next step is to check the deployment configuration for the frontend service to see if the command is correctly set. \n\nWait, the deployment for frontend might have a command specified. The user can check the deployment\'s YAML to see the command and args. So the next step would be to get the deployment\'s YAML using kubectl. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the image is incorrect. The user could check the image\'s existence and contents. But without access to the image repository, maybe they can\'t. \n\nSo the next tool to use would be "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd" to get the deployment\'s YAML for the frontend service. That way, they can check the command and args specified in the deployment, which might be pointing to the wrong executable or missing arguments. \n\nAlternatively, check the pod\'s container details again. But the describe pod already showed the command as "frontend". \n\nSo the conclusion is that the frontend image is missing the executable, or the entry point is wrong. The user needs to verify the image\'s contents or the deployment\'s command. Since the tools don\'t allow inspecting the image, the next step is to check the deployment configuration to see if the command is correctly set. \n\nTherefore, the next tool call should be to get the deployment\'s YAML for the frontend service.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The frontend container failed because the executable "frontend" was not found in the $PATH. This suggests the container image might be missing the required binary or the entry point/command is misconfigured. To verify, I need to inspect the deployment configuration for the f