e previous command used "frontend" as the container name, which doesn\'t match. The correct container name is "hotel-reserv-frontend".\n\nSo the next step is to correct the image in the deployment using the right container name. The user needs to run the `kubectl set image` command again, this time specifying the correct container name. The command should be `kubectl set image deployment/frontend frontend=yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest -n hotel-reservation`, but wait, the container name in the YAML is "hotel-reserv-frontend", not "frontend". So the correct command should use "hotel-reserv-frontend" as the container name. \n\nWait, in the deployment YAML, the container\'s name is "hotel-reserv-frontend". So the correct command should be `kubectl set image deployment/frontend hotel-reserv-frontend=yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest -n hotel-reservation`. That way, it targets the correct container. The user previously used "frontend" as the container name, which was wrong. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to execute this corrected command. Then, after updating the image, they should check if the pod is using the new image by describing the pod again. If the image is updated, the pod should start correctly. If there are any other issues, like the image not being available, they might need to pull it or check the registry. But the main issue now is the correct container name in the image update command.\n</think>\n\n**Tool Choice:** `exec_kubectl_cmd_safely`  \n**Reasoning:** The deployment\'s container name is `hotel-reserv-frontend`, but the previous command used `frontend` as the container name, causing the error. We need to explicitly specify the correct container name in the `kubectl set image` command to update the image to `yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest`.  \n\n```json\n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl set image deployment/frontend hotel-reserv-frontend=yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest -n hotel-reservation"\n  }\n}\n```', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 599, 'prompt_tokens': 6822, 'total_tokens': 7421, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfac2-e8a8-7161-b1d5-43cc595dcd0a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6822, 'output_tokens': 599, 'total_tokens': 7421}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='657b44b8-ff76-4b30-9c1a-ca3185c0f692'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to fix an issue with the frontend service in their Kubernetes cluster. The problem seems to be that the container image specified in the deployment is incorrect. \n\nFirst, they checked the pods and saw that the frontend pod was in a CrashLoopBackOff state. When they looked at the logs, the error was about the executable 'frontend' not being found. That suggests the container image doesn't have the necessary binary.\n\nThen, they used kubectl to get the deployment details and found that the image was set to 'jackcuii/hotel-reservation:latest', but the correct image should be 'yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest'. The container name in the deployment is 'hotel-reserv-frontend', which they initially missed when trying to update the image.\n\nThe user tried to set the image using 'kubectl set image deployment/frontend frontend=yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest', but that failed because the container name is actually 'hotel-reserv-frontend'. So the correct command should use the right container name.\n\nNow, they're about to call the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool again with the corrected container name. This should update the deployment to use the correct image. After that, they might need to wait for the deployment to rollout and check if the pods are running correctly. If everything works, the issue should be resolved. If not, they might need to check the logs again or verify the image availability.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl set image deployment/frontend hotel-reserv-frontend=yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-81ec98cb2616d534', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 361, 'prompt_tokens': 7985, 'total_tokens': 8346, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfac3-05c6-74b1-9159-6d16d3e796f0-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl set image deployment/frontend hotel-reserv-frontend=yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-81ec98cb2616d534', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7985, 'output_tokens': 361, 'total_tokens': 8346}), ToolMessage(content='deployment.apps/frontend image updated\n', id='3177e9a6-33b4-4427-ab0d-b5c759789646', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-81ec98cb2616d534'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='60966bd9-0ba2-4608-89c8-93bef177b4a2')]]
